  "step_3": "The decision reason requires computing outcome differences from a reference value (here, -3000) and evaluating lotteries based on the mean of these differences. The individual does not perform such calculations, nor do they mention differences from a reference point in numerical or conceptual terms. However, they do treat the sure loss in B as a psychological anchor, contrasting A’s possibility of zero loss against it — which aligns with the general idea of a reference-dependent evaluation. While the qualitative emphasis on avoiding a certain loss resonates with the framework of the decision reason (especially loss aversion or reference dependence), the absence of any consideration of weighted differences or systematic evaluation of deviations from the reference suggests the full reasoning process is not applied. The individual focuses on the positive aspect of a zero loss in A relative to B, which is consistent with part of the reason’s logic, but they do not integrate all differences by probability as required. Thus, there is partial alignment in spirit but not in method.",
